          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"stendhal-le-rouge-et-le-noir-3b3ce51fe2c1c280\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"Julien directly presents his past love for Mme de Rênal as a fact and as grounds for deserving her confidence. The utterance is primarily a retrospective avowal used to support a demand for information. It does not itself undertake a relational commitment, and its emotional intensity supplies no independent evidence of expressive/reflexive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The perfect tense and « assez » frame the love as prior and sufficient for a present claim, without clearly stating whether it continues; this temporal nuance does not alter the dominant truth-conditional force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Julien directly addresses Mme de Rênal: « je vous ai assez aimée pour mériter cette confidence ».\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"After forcing a nocturnal reunion following fourteen months of separation, Julien invokes the extent of his former love to justify his demand that Mme de Rênal disclose everything that has happened. The narration explicitly characterizes his delivery as authoritative and shows that it influences her. This coercive or strategically pressuring context affects the use of the avowal but does not change its core truth-conditional mode into a performative undertaking or reflexive discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"« Ah! je vous ai assez aimée pour mériter cette confidence... »\",\"supports\":\"The perfect-tense declaration explicitly presents Julien's having loved Mme de Rênal sufficiently as true, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The avowal is embedded between « Je veux savoir tout ce que vous avez fait » and « Je veux tout savoir. »\",\"supports\":\"The utterance serves as a reason for Julien's demand for disclosure rather than as a promise or relational undertaking, supporting P=0.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"« Malgré Mme de Rênal, ce ton d'autorité avait de l'empire sur son coeur. »\",\"supports\":\"The narration frames the delivery as authoritative and influential; this explains its interpersonal pressure but does not establish expressive/reflexive core force.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Soon afterward the narration describes Julien's « transport d'amour » and says that everything had disappeared from his heart except love.\",\"supports\":\"The surrounding narration corroborates love as a genuine represented emotional state, although truth-conditional classification does not depend on sincerity.\",\"confidence\":0.96}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the retrospective avowal; its use as leverage and its authoritative delivery are adequately handled as context, with no residual core force requiring O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
